//? Title: Mersenne Twister: Reproducible Words and Recoverable State
//? Subtitle: MT19937-64 twist arithmetic, inverse tempering and honest empirical checks
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A simulation needs a long, repeatable stream of numeric inputs from a compact seed,
with a precise contract for restarting and transforming that stream. MT19937-64
produces 64-bit words from a 312-word state array. We work through a single twist
and a tiny tempering example before checking seeded streams across many refills.
Inverting tempering recovers a continuation from one aligned block of full outputs,
showing why reproducibility and a long period do not imply unpredictability.
Single-stream and forty-seed statistical checks then describe selected finite samples,
without treating unremarkable p-values as a certification of randomness.
)
\toc

> The method

>> A seed is a starting condition, not fresh entropy

`dm::random::MersenneTwister64(seed)` owns its state and implements **MT19937-64**.
It matches the scalar-seeded raw stream of `std::mt19937_64`, not the 32-bit
`std::mt19937` engine or an unspecified language's default random generator.
The 64-bit version has distinct parameters and output. In particular the original
32-bit paper's 623-dimensional statement must not simply be attached to this engine.

For \(n=312\) words, scalar initialisation uses unsigned wraparound:

$$
 x_0=s,\qquad x_i=6364136223846793005\,
 [x_{i-1}\oplus(x_{i-1}\gg62)]+i\pmod{2^{64}},\quad 1\le i<312.
$$
\alttext(The seed is word zero. Each later word multiplies the previous word XOR its right shift by 62, adds its index, and retains the low 64 bits.)

For seed zero, the first three words are zero, one and 6364136223846793007:
seeding zero does not initialise an all-zero array. The first output refills the
array before reading it. `block_index()` is 312 initially, then one after that
first output. Copies preserve both the raw array/index and the inherited normal
sampler's cache. `state_words()` alone is a borrowed raw view, not a full checkpoint;
a later refill changes that view.

There are 19968 stored word bits, but the recurrence's effective degree is
\(312\times64-31=19937\). Its published nonzero-state period is
\(2^{19937}-1\). This is a property of the chosen recurrence and its characteristic
polynomial, not a conclusion from the short runs below. A scalar seed supplies
at most \(2^{64}\) possible initial streams, not 19937 bits of entropy. Different
seeds do not prove independent streams. No fresh randomness arrives after seeding.

>> Join two words and apply the twist

Let \(U=\mathtt{FFFFFFFF80000000}\), \(L=\mathtt{000000007FFFFFFF}\), and
\(A=\mathtt{B5026F5AA96619E9}\), all hexadecimal 64-bit constants. In recurrence
order, one new word is

$$
 v=(x_i\mathbin{\&}U)\mathbin{|}(x_{i+1}\mathbin{\&}L),\qquad
 x_{i+312}=x_{i+156}\oplus(v\gg1)\oplus
 \begin{cases}A,&v\text{ odd},\\0,&v\text{ even}.\end{cases}
$$
\alttext(Join the upper 33 bits of the left word with the lower 31 bits of the next word. XOR the lag-156 word with that join shifted right once and, when the join is odd, the twist mask A.)

The join has disjoint bit fields, so its OR also equals XOR. The odd correction
is the low bit of v multiplied by the fixed binary vector A; thus the twist is
linear over \(\mathbf F_2\). This does not make the scalar-seeding recurrence,
which includes integer multiplication and addition, binary-linear.

The implementation fills its ring **in increasing index order**. Steps 0–155
read lag words from the old block; steps 156–311 use already replaced lag words.
The final step also takes the lower bits of the newly written word zero. A loop
that computes all 312 updates simultaneously from a frozen old array implements
a different stream. Refill costs O(312); between refills a draw reads and tempers
one word, giving O(1) amortised cost and fixed storage. The array itself is
2496 bytes, with additional index, distribution cache and object metadata.

The retained diagram needs LaTeX/TikZ, amsmath, lmodern and dvisvgm to rebuild.
Ordinary documentation export consumes the saved local SVG.

```{bash, file="figures/mt64_stream_mechanism.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/mt64_stream_mechanism.tikz > build/mt64_stream_mechanism.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/mt64_stream_mechanism.svg build/mt64_stream_mechanism.dvi > build/mt64_stream_mechanism.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the seed, ordered-refill and output-transform diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the seed, ordered-refill and output-transform diagram.
// \image(figures/mt64_stream_mechanism.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The state transition and the output transform have different roles; only seeding chooses a starting point.)
\alttext(A seed fills 312 words. An ordered twist refills the block when its index is exhausted. Each selected raw word passes through invertible tempering to a full 64-bit output. Keeping its upper 53 bits produces a grid-valued uniform sample; tempering does not conceal the state word.)

>> Tempering is an invertible output map

Starting from one raw word, apply the four operations below in order, truncating
left shifts to 64 bits. The native inspection function returns the input and all
four intermediate words.

$$
 \begin{aligned}
 z_1&=x\oplus[(x\gg29)\mathbin{\&}\mathtt{5555555555555555}],\\
 z_2&=z_1\oplus[(z_1\ll17)\mathbin{\&}\mathtt{71D67FFFEDA60000}],\\
 z_3&=z_2\oplus[(z_2\ll37)\mathbin{\&}\mathtt{FFF7EEE000000000}],\\
 y&=z_3\oplus(z_3\gg43).
 \end{aligned}
$$
\alttext(Tempering applies right-29 masked XOR, left-17 masked XOR, left-37 masked XOR, and finally right-43 XOR. Each stage uses the preceding stage's value.)

For any one stage write \(y=(I+N)x\), where N is its masked-shift map over
\(\mathbf F_2\). Every application shifts surviving information farther in one
direction, so \(N^q=0\) once \(q\,\text{shift}\ge64\). Therefore
\((I+N)^{-1}=I+N+\cdots+N^{q-1}\): multiplying the two expressions cancels every
interior term, leaving \(I+N^q=I\). Undo the four stages in reverse order.
This proves a unique preimage for every 64-bit output, and explains the native
`mt19937_64_untemper` implementation. Tempering changes distribution properties
of the output coordinates without increasing period, state entropy or secrecy.

The common `uniform01()` sampler takes the upper 53 bits and multiplies by
\(2^{-53}\). Its possible values are exactly \(j/2^{53}\), from zero through
\(1-2^{-53}\); it is a discrete grid rather than a literal continuous uniform.
It discards eleven bits, so the simple full-word inversion shown here does not
apply directly to those floating samples. Integer `uniform_u64` uses unbiased
multiply-and-reject range mapping; `normal` uses cached Box–Muller pairs.
Consequently a seed alone does not specify a mixed sampling transcript: the call
order, parameters and cached state matter. Raw integer streams are exact; results
involving log, sin and cos can vary with the floating-point math implementation.

> Small worked examples

>> One odd bit changes an entire next word

Supply left word \(\mathtt{8000000000000000}\), lag word zero, and right word
zero or one. The join is respectively \(\mathtt{8000000000000000}\) or
\(\mathtt{8000000000000001}\). Both right shifts equal
\(\mathtt{4000000000000000}\), but the odd case XORs A and becomes
\(\mathtt{F5026F5AA96619E9}\). The table preserves exact hex words instead of
putting 64-bit integers into floating-point DataFrame columns. In each raster,
row zero is the least significant bit and a dark cell means one.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/mt64_twist.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto even = dm::random::mt19937_64_twist_steps(0x8000000000000000ULL, 0, 0);
  const auto odd  = dm::random::mt19937_64_twist_steps(0x8000000000000000ULL, 1, 0);
  const std::vector<std::string> stages{"join", "shift", "mask", "result"};
  auto a = dm::random::bit_plot(std::vector<std::uint64_t>(even.begin(), even.end()), 64)
               .title("Even join: no correction")
               .x_ticks({.5, 1.5, 2.5, 3.5}, stages)
               .x_label("twist stage");
  auto b = dm::random::bit_plot(std::vector<std::uint64_t>(odd.begin(), odd.end()), 64)
               .title("Odd join: XOR the twist mask")
               .x_ticks({.5, 1.5, 2.5, 3.5}, stages)
               .x_label("twist stage");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(a).add(b).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/mt64_twist.svg");
  std::print("| stage | even case | odd case |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < stages.size(); ++i)
    std::print("| {} | {:016x} | {:016x} |\n", stages[i], even[i], odd[i]);
}
```
