\caption(A full aligned block recovers the raw continuation, with no held-out mismatches.)
\alttext(The actual and predicted low-bit rasters agree exactly on 64 unseen outputs. The printed mismatch count covers 4096 subsequent words across thirteen complete refills and part of the next.)

>> A finite sample can look uniform and remain predictable

The existing seven-test battery checks 100-cell uniform counts, uniform KS,
non-overlapping 10-by-10 serial cells, lag-one correlation, low/high monobit counts
and low-byte frequencies. Each stage uses fresh words in a fixed order; the two
monobit counts share their n words. The full battery advances the generator by
7n raw outputs. Set n=20000 before inspecting the results.

For a bit with ideal independent fair draws, the estimated one-frequency has
standard deviation \(1/(2\sqrt n)\). The plotted three-SD guides are **pointwise**,
not simultaneous 64-bit acceptance bands. The histogram uses another fresh engine
with the same seed; these reused prefixes are reproducible, not independent
replications. The null reference is a modelling benchmark for the diagnostics,
not a claim that a deterministic seeded sequence literally consists of independent
random variables.

The chi-square statistics use their multinomial degrees of freedom. Correlation
and monobit p-values use approximate normal tails; the KS routine uses a corrected
asymptotic reference. The floating uniform's very fine grid, dependence and finite
sample size qualify those calibrations. No rule here declares a generator good
because every p-value exceeds 0.05: even ideal calibrated tests sometimes give
small p-values, and these tests are not mutually independent.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/mt64_quality.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  constexpr std::size_t         n = 20000;
  dm::random::MersenneTwister64 battery(5489), bits(5489), uniforms(5489);
  const auto                    tests       = dm::random::quality_tests(battery, n);
  const auto                    frequencies = dm::random::bit_frequencies(bits, n);
  const auto                    u           = dm::random::uniform_sample(uniforms, n);
  const double                  delta       = 3 / (2 * std::sqrt(double(n)));
  auto                          a           = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
               .points(dm::seql(0, 63, 64).values(), frequencies, "frequency", {40, 100, 190}, 2)
               .abline_h(.5, {130, 130, 130}, 1)
               .abline_h(.5 - delta, {190, 100, 35}, 1)
               .abline_h(.5 + delta, {190, 100, 35}, 1)
               .title("20000 words: per-bit balance")
               .x_label("bit position (0 = lowest)")
               .y_label("fraction of ones")
               .x_limits(0, 63)
               .y_limits(.48, .535)
               .legend(
                   {"frequency", "fair-bit mean", "pointwise +/-3 SD"},
                   {{40, 100, 190}, {130, 130, 130}, {190, 100, 35}});
  auto b = dm::graphics::RPlot::hist(u, 20, "uniforms", {40, 100, 190})
               .title("Same-seed uniforms: 20 cells")
               .x_label("upper-53-bit uniform")
               .y_label("count")
               .x_limits(0, 1)
               .y_limits(0, 1400);
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(a).add(b).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/mt64_quality.svg");
  std::print("{}\n", tests.to_md(6));
}
```
