\image(figures/mt64_quality.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Balance and low-dimensional goodness-of-fit tests describe this sample without resolving predictability.)
\alttext(Sixty-four one-frequencies lie near one half, with pointwise three-SD guides. A twenty-cell uniform histogram is approximately flat. These simple diagnostics coexist with exact recovery from full outputs.)

The single-seed low-byte test gives p=0.035514. It is retained alongside the
other six results. An isolated value below 0.05 calls for context and a declared
follow-up protocol; it is neither suppressed nor treated as conclusive evidence
from an independent, perfectly calibrated family of tests.

>> Repeat the battery across forty declared seeds

A second-level check looks at the first-level p-values across seeds 1 through 40,
with n=20000 per stage. Under an ideal independent, exactly calibrated reference,
first-level p-values would be uniform; one expects about two below 0.05 per test,
with standard deviation \(\sqrt{40(0.05)(0.95)}\), about 1.38. The figure's
horizontal count-two guide is an expectation, not a threshold. The native table
also reports upper-tail counts, mean p and a KS check of each p-value distribution.
Finite-sample approximations, discrete statistics and consecutive-seed dependence
mean the second-level p-values are diagnostic rather than exact significance
levels. Seven second-level comparisons add another multiplicity issue. We retain
all seven outcomes rather than selecting a favourable test or seed.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/mt64_many_seeds.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto table = dm::random::quality_tests_over_seeds(
      [](std::uint64_t seed) { return std::make_unique<dm::random::MersenneTwister64>(seed); },
      40,
      20000,
      1);
  const std::vector<std::string> labels{
      "cells", "KS", "pairs", "lag1", "low bit", "high bit", "byte"};
  auto a = dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(
               table.numeric_column("small_p_count").values(), labels, "count", {40, 100, 190})
               .abline_h(2, {190, 100, 35}, 1)
               .title("Forty seeds: p below 0.05")
               .x_label("first-level test")
               .y_label("number of small p-values")
               .y_limits(0, 10)
               .legend({"observed", "ideal expectation"}, {{40, 100, 190}, {190, 100, 35}});
  auto b = dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(
               table.numeric_column("ks_p_value").values(), labels, "KS p", {70, 145, 90})
               .title("Second-level KS diagnostics")
               .x_label("first-level test")
               .y_label("approximate KS p-value")
               .y_limits(0, 1.15);
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(a).add(b).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/mt64_many_seeds.svg");
  std::print("{}\n", table.to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| test | seeds | draws_per_test | mean_p | small_p_count | large_p_count | ks_statistic | ks_p_value |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Uniformity, chi-square (100 cells) | 40 | 20000 | 0.516464 | 1 | 2 | 0.074607 | 0.974250 |
| Uniformity, Kolmogorov-Smirnov | 40 | 20000 | 0.509335 | 1 | 0 | 0.139834 | 0.387777 |
| Serial pairs, chi-square (10 x 10 cells) | 40 | 20000 | 0.545065 | 3 | 7 | 0.138229 | 0.402152 |
| Lag-1 correlation, z = r sqrt(n) | 40 | 20000 | 0.451036 | 4 | 4 | 0.147284 | 0.325351 |
| Monobit, lowest bit (z) | 40 | 20000 | 0.528789 | 2 | 4 | 0.105635 | 0.739954 |
| Monobit, highest bit (z) | 40 | 20000 | 0.483400 | 3 | 3 | 0.091843 | 0.872788 |
| Lowest byte, chi-square (256 cells) | 40 | 20000 | 0.438160 | 1 | 1 | 0.157814 | 0.249392 |

\image(figures/mt64_many_seeds.svg)
// result_end
\caption(All seven second-level results are retained; neither the count guide nor a p-value certifies the engine.)
\alttext(For forty consecutive seeds, bars show counts of first-level p-values below 0.05 and second-level KS p-values. The count-two line is the ideal expected count, with substantial sampling variation.)

The serial-pair test has seven p-values above 0.95 across these forty seeds,
compared with the ideal expected count two. Its second-level KS p-value is
0.402152: a global CDF discrepancy and a selected tail count measure different
features. This visible excess is a reason to retain the whole table and, if
using the diagnostic for a decision, plan a larger follow-up rather than change
the seed range after inspection.

> What the examples establish

The twist example checks exact masking and conditional XOR. The tempering example
connects intermediate words to a finite inverse-series proof. Seeded conformance
checks exercise the ordered refill and the standard's long transcript rather than
relying on one matching output. The continuation example shows that 312 aligned
full words reveal enough state to predict later words exactly, despite balanced
bits and a huge recurrence period.

Statistical plots and forty-seed checks expose only selected low-dimensional
features of finite samples. They do not prove the stated period, all-dimensional
equidistribution, stream independence or cryptographic security. MT19937-64 is
appropriate here as a reproducible simulation mechanism; a secret seed does not
turn its invertible output mapping into protection for adversarial use.
No external dataset is needed for this algorithm report: all inputs, reference
words and observed sequences are declared, and correctness has an exact oracle.
No timing ranking or practical throughput claim is inferred from the complexity
calculation.

> References

- M. Matsumoto and T. Nishimura (1998), “Mersenne twister: A 623-dimensionally
  equidistributed uniform pseudo-random number generator”, *ACM Transactions on
  Modeling and Computer Simulation* 8(1), 3–30.
  [Original 32-bit paper](https://doi.org/10.1145/272991.272995).
- [Authors' MT19937-64 page](https://math.sci.hiroshima-u.ac.jp/m-mat/MT/emt64.html),
  with the distinct 64-bit implementation and parameters.
- C++ working draft: [Mersenne Twister transition, seeding and output specification](https://eel.is/c++draft/rand.eng.mers)
  and [predefined MT19937-64 parameters and 10000th reference word](https://eel.is/c++draft/rand.predef).
  These specify the comparison engine; the inspection and inversion arguments above
  are derived directly from those word operations.

